Derek Ray wrote:

 > USENET has been doomed and/or dying for a long
 > while.

People who have no understanding of Usenet have been
saying that since at least 1984, when I joined. The
greater text "store and forward" net surely is not
used by everyone today, since the hypertext-based
GlitzNet's appearance in 1992, but it is used by
hundreds of millions of people around the world
regularly, and, last I looked, a very long time ago,
had passed 120,000 newsgroups.

That an individual newsgroup's traffic level should
wax and wane within this larger ocean of postings is
of no particular importance, and in the case of
rgrn, episodes of 30 or more postings a day for
months at a time are separated by lnog fallow
periods, and that's been true for at least a decade.

Be grateful for that. Some newsgroups, like
talk.origins, have so much traffic you cannot follow
the entire discussion in real time even working at
it 16 hours per day.

Let the doom-sayers go elsewhere, otherwise ignore
them. This newsgroup will persist as long as nethack
attracts new players.

The comment about better interfaces is valid,
though. Google Groups is cute because it allows you
to give ratings to postings, and because it is
backed up by the deepest archive available, but is
so buggy it is an embarrassment to Google Inc.

I use http://www.albasani.net's reader.albasani.net
as my (free) news provider and Mozilla's Thunderbird
as my (free) news client. Neither is ideal, but they
are "good enough" for most purposes.

